<DocsLayout title="Delegated agent access">
<h1>Delegated agent access</h1>
<p>This optional OAuth-only flow lets a native public client act as an owned
agent after explicit human consent. It is off by default. It issues no ID token
and never turns an agent into a human sign-in account.</p>
<h2>Enable deliberately</h2>
<p>Enable agent accounts and session revocation, set the issuer to an HTTPS origin
without a path, query, fragment or trailing slash, and configure
<code>GATEWAY_AGENT_OAUTH_RESOURCE_CREDENTIALS_JSON</code> as a bounded JSON array of
<code>id</code>, <code>project_id</code>, <code>resource</code> and
<code>secret_sha256</code>. Generate a high-entropy resource secret outside source
control; Identity stores its SHA-256 digest while the resource keeps the secret.
No public client ID or directory credential substitutes for that secret.</p>
<p>Register a native client with exact redirects, one or more resource/scope
bounds and a <code>product</code> policy slug. A registration grants no permission.
Then enable <code>GATEWAY_AGENT_OAUTH_ENABLED</code> only after completing the
runnable <code>examples/agent-delegation</code> journey.</p>
<h2>Complete the journey</h2>
<ol>
<li>The native client creates an unpredictable state and verifier, then opens
<code>/oauth/authorize</code> with response_type=code, its client ID, exact
redirect, one resource, requested scopes and the S256 challenge.</li>
<li>The hosted <code>/auth/agent-consent</code> page uses normal human sign-in.
Consent requires a live human SID and a sign-in within five minutes. Review
client, resource, scopes and expiry, then explicitly select an owned active
agent and approve or deny. Signing in does not itself approve access.</li>
<li>The client verifies the callback state and issuer, then redeems the code at
<code>/oauth/token</code> using the original verifier and exact original bounds.
A code is single-use and expires after one minute. It receives an agent access
JWT and, if registered, an opaque rotating refresh credential.</li>
<li>The resource verifies the distinct <code>at+jwt</code> profile and authenticates
to <code>/oauth/introspect</code> using HTTP Basic on every request. It checks
active, issuer, project, single audience, scopes and actor/agent bindings. On
an inactive token or an unavailable authority service it refuses access.</li>
<li>The human can list and revoke their grants through the session RPCs.
The native client can revoke its refresh credential at <code>/oauth/revoke</code>.
A used refresh token replay terminally revokes the whole grant.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Hosted sign-in choices</h2>
<p>The review page offers only providers enabled for the current project. Provider
sign-in also requires <code>GATEWAY_OAUTH_ALLOWED_RETURN_URLS</code> to admit the
actual consent page, such as <code>https://auth.example.com/auth/agent-consent</code>.
An unlisted page explains that hosted sign-in is unavailable; it does not offer
broken provider links. Hub-shared providers start on their configured origin and
need the original project routing key.</p>
<p>The browser binds each return to its original tab, request and project using a
short-lived nonsecret intent. It removes that intent before redeeming a one-time
code and keeps tokens only in page memory. A canceled provider sign-in returns
safely only after server state and browser CSRF checks. Signing in never selects
an agent or approves access. Password and its TOTP challenge remain available
only when enabled; hosted redemption that requires a TOTP challenge currently
fails closed because that response has no challenge fields.</p>
<h2>Bounds and loss of authority</h2>
<p>Access lasts 300 seconds by default (maximum 900); consent defaults to 86400
seconds (maximum thirty days); refresh idle defaults to 3600 seconds. Original
human session lifetime and tenant idle/absolute policy can shorten these bounds.
Delegation does not keep a human session active. Ownership changes, signout,
status or credential changes, guardian loss, client edits and project changes
invalidate old grants. Restoring a previous owner or enabling a client again
never restores the old grant. Product and age policy derives from the current
human owner and the registered product, never a caller-supplied age claim.</p>
<p>Only GET authorization and form-encoded POST token/introspection/revocation
are exposed. Requests are limited to 64 KiB and compact access JWTs to 32 KiB;
the configured issuer is at most 2048 bytes. The <code>openid</code> scope is
explicitly refused. Duplicate parameters, body/query mixing, client secrets on public
clients, non-S256 proof, multiple resources and scope widening are refused.
Discovery is <code>/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server</code>; there is no
OIDC discovery, userinfo, implicit flow, dynamic registration or generic mint API.</p>
<p>Native-gRPC hosts that call consent RPCs must explicitly opt into
<code>GRPCTrustSessionID</code> and <code>GRPCTrustAuthTime</code> only after their
verified interceptor owns those metadata values. Otherwise the bridge strips
them. Ordinary existing gRPC auth behavior is unchanged.</p>
<h2>Durable unattended machines</h2>
<p>For an orchestrator that must survive human signout, enable the separate
<code>GATEWAY_AGENT_MACHINE_ENABLED</code> feature and explicitly register
<code>agent_machine</code> in the native client's grant types. This is a typed
RPC capability, separate from the browser OAuth flow above.</p>
<p>The signed-in owner calls <code>RegisterAgentMachine</code> with exact bounds
and the engine's Ed25519 public key (64 lowercase hex characters). The engine
creates and protects the private key locally; Identity never receives it. Persist
the request ID and key pair before sending. Identical retries recover the result.
Omitted selection includes all current and future owned agents; Team and
application permissions still apply.</p>
<p>The engine key can discover eligible agents and register their separate public
keys through <code>RegisterAgentMachineAgentKey</code>. Only each agent's key may
use <code>ExchangeAgentMachineCredential</code> to obtain its own short-lived token.
Each operation first obtains a <code>CreateAgentMachineChallenge</code> and signs
its exact message. Challenges bind issuer, project, machine, agent, operation and
request, expire after 60 seconds and can be consumed once across issuer restarts.
After response loss, obtain a fresh challenge; do not replay a consumed proof.</p>
<p><code>ListAgentMachineAgents</code> pages the selected owned agents. Continue
until <code>next_offset</code> is -1, even when a filtered page is empty. Its proof
is bound to that page offset. Keep all private keys outside agent workspaces;
pass each agent only its own access token. New eligible agents can enroll after
the owner's human session ends. Existing agent keys cannot be silently replaced.</p>
<p>Owners list, select and revoke connections with <code>ListAgentMachines</code>,
<code>SelectAgentMachineAgents</code> and <code>RevokeAgentMachine</code>.
Selection changes require recent human sign-in and an expected revision.
Excluded or revoked child grants never reconnect through default-all. Machine
revocation stops all children. Replacement requires a newly authorized connection.
Human signout alone does not revoke this kind; ownership, status, security,
client and policy loss still do. Resources keep introspecting on every request.
Resources authenticate with their dedicated project/resource HTTP Basic
credential to <code>IntrospectAgentMachine</code> on every machine-control request;
a public client ID or human bearer cannot substitute for verification.
No application machine policy or existing expiring approval is silently extended.</p>
